  },
  "holistic_F_N": 2,
  "holistic_F_H": -2,
  "section_notes": "The final text sets the S2 permanent failure cap at 7% (above the 5% answer-key limit), which keeps Northwind's Hearth 10 (6.2%) on the market, and gives per-model rates to customers while withholding them from the public. The S5 mandatory recall trigger is 8 per 100,000 per model with no transition, which would catch Halcyon's Ember 8 (9.9). S3 sets new-unit shut-off at 24 months and retrofits at 6 years, and S4 sets built-in suppression at 36 months. The S1 reporting rules (24-hour reporting, 72-hour customer notice, all incidents) are the strongest provisions."
 },
